<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">International Railways of Central America</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as IRCA</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">International Railways of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> (IRCA) was a United States-owned railway company formed in 1912 that controlled most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>'s railways as well as lines in El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>. Closely linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-fruit-company">United Fruit Company</a>, which became a major shareholder, it ran the line from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> to Puerto Barrios on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and used its monopoly over freight to dominate the country's export trade for decades.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/international-railways-of-central-america.jpg" alt="International Railways of Central America" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ferrocarril_del_norte2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
